It walks the directory tree, and for each supported file it extracts the text and reports every hit with its location. 
The extraction is per-format: PDFs give you the page number, Excel gives you Sheet!Cell (across all sheets), 
Word gives you the paragraph or table-cell index, and plain text gives you the line number. One bad or unreadable 
file gets reported to stderr and the run keeps going rather than crashing.

python docsearch.py "invoice number"                # search current dir
python docsearch.py "patient" --path ./records      # a specific folder
python docsearch.py "INV-\d+" --regex               # regex mode
python docsearch.py "Confidential" --case-sensitive
python docsearch.py "acme" --ext pdf,xlsx           # limit file types
python docsearch.py "acme" --files-only             # grep -l style

Covered types: .docx, .pdf, .xlsx/.xlsm/.xls, plus .txt/.csv/.md/.log/.tsv as a freebie since they're trivial. 
Adding .pptx (via python-docx's sibling python-pptx) is a five-line extractor if you want it.

Two real limits worth knowing up front, both of which the script tells you about rather than failing silently. 
Legacy .doc (the old binary Word format) can't be read by python-docx — convert to .docx first, e.g. soffice --headless --
convert-to docx *.doc on your WSL side. And scanned/image PDFs have no embedded text layer, so they'll come back flagged as
"no extractable text (needs OCR)" — you'd run something like ocrmypdf over them first, then re-search.

